1
00:00:00,420 --> 00:00:02,477
Book Four, Proposition Seven.

2
00:00:02,477 --> 00:00:05,739
About a given circle to circumscribe a square.

3
00:00:05,739 --> 00:00:12,690
Let A B C D be the given circle; it is required to circumscribe a square about the circle A B C D.

4
00:00:13,110 --> 00:00:21,793
Draw two diameters A C and B D of the circle A B C D at right angles to one another through the center E.

5
00:00:21,793 --> 00:00:31,800
Through the points A, B, C, and D draw F G, G H, H K, and K F touching the circle A B C D by the Porism to Three Sixteen.

6
00:00:32,220 --> 00:00:45,028
Because F G touches the circle and radius E A is joined to the point of contact, by Three Eighteen the angles at A are right; for the same reason the angles at B, C, and D are also right.

7
00:00:45,028 --> 00:00:57,493
Since angles A E B and E B G are both right, by One Twenty-Eight and One Thirty the tangents G H and F K are parallel to diameter A C, while F G and H K are parallel to diameter B D.

8
00:00:57,493 --> 00:01:15,096
Thus the figures are parallelograms, so by One Thirty-Four each side equals the equal diameters A C and B D, making F G H K equilateral; and because opposite angles in parallelogram G B E A are equal, angle A G B and the angles at H, K, and F are all right.

9
00:01:15,096 --> 00:01:19,822
Therefore F G H K is a square circumscribed about the circle A B C D.

10
00:01:19,822 --> 00:01:21,260
Which was to be done.

11
00:01:21,680 --> 00:01:24,849
Proposition Four Seven is the tangent dual of Four Six.

12
00:01:24,849 --> 00:01:42,019
As Heath remarks in his commentary, any quadrilateral circumscribed about a circle must have the sum of one pair of opposite sides equal to the sum of the other pair—Pitot's theorem—so among equiangular quadrilaterals only a square is similar to a given rectangle when circumscribed about a circle.

13
00:01:42,019 --> 00:01:55,040
In the GEOMETOR G-Index, node G one hundred fifty-nine unites Book Three tangent theory with Book One parallelogram theorems across forty-nine ancestor nodes, serving alongside Four Six in the exhaustion proofs of Book Twelve.

14
